# Would you pay a fortune for an infinitely valuable game? The St Petersburg paradox
tags: ["st petersburg paradox","expected value","expected utility","daniel bernoulli","decision theory"]
teaches: ["concepts/st-petersburg-paradox","concepts/expected-utility","concepts/diminishing-marginal-utility"]
voices: {"a":"gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed","b":"gemini:Aoede | a relaxed, good-humoured co-host with a dry, understated wit; never exaggerated; speaks at a slightly brisk pace","take":"lines"}
prompt: "Why is the expected payout of a simple coin game infinite? Why will nobody pay much to play — and what did Bernoulli conclude about utility?"
next: {"title":"One box or two? Newcomb's problem","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/paradoxes/11-one-box-or-two-newcomb-s-problem.cast"}

## The St Petersburg paradox
vars: {"you":10}
sources:
    - id: bernoulli1738
      title: Specimen theoriae novae de mensura sortis (Exposition of a new theory on the measurement of risk)
      authors: Daniel Bernoulli
      year: 1738
      finding: Value a gamble by the utility of wealth, not its money; logarithmic utility gives the St Petersburg game a small finite price (Commentarii Academiae Scientiarum Imperialis Petropolitanae 5; English translation by L. Sommer, Econometrica 1954).
    - id: sep
      title: The St. Petersburg Paradox (Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy)
      authors: Martin Peterson
      year: 2019
      finding: Nicolaus Bernoulli posed the problem to Montmort in 1713; Cramer (1728) and Daniel Bernoulli (1738) proposed diminishing utility; Menger (1934) restored the paradox for unbounded utility.
      url: https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/paradox-stpetersburg/
    - id: menger1934
      title: Das Unsicherheitsmoment in der Wertlehre
      authors: Karl Menger
      year: 1934
      finding: 'For any unbounded utility, prizes that grow fast enough give infinite expected utility again (Zeitschrift für Nationalökonomie 5: 459–485).'
    - id: vnm1944
      title: Theory of Games and Economic Behavior
      authors: John von Neumann and Oskar Morgenstern
      year: 1944
      finding: Axioms for choice under risk from which expected-utility maximisation follows.

