    card title "Would you pay a fortune?"

A: How much would you pay to play a coin game once?
    draw coin header

A: I toss a fair coin until it lands heads. Heads on th (@draw row1@)e very first toss, and you win two dollars.

A: Every tail before it doubles the prize: heads on the sec (@draw row2@)ond toss pays four, on (@draw row3@) the third (@draw row4@) eight, then si (@draw r5@)xteen, and on it goes.

A: The game always ends, usually fast, and you can't lose anything but the price you paid to play.
    highlight prizes

    ask "What's the most you'd pay to play once? Type a price in dollars." store price default "10" voice a

    animate you "{price}" duration 0.1

A: Hold on to your number. By the textbook rule for pricing a bet, this gam (@draw teaser@)e is worth more than all the money in the world.
    draw you

A: It's an old puzzle. Nicolaus (@draw tag_n@) Bernoulli posed a version of it in a letter in 1713; his cousin  (@draw tag_d@)Daniel published the famous answer in St Petersburg in 1 (@draw tag_sp@)738, which gave the paradox its name.

    erase tag_n tag_d tag_sp teaser

A: Our route: work out the game's average payout, see why nobody believes it, then find the innocent-looking step that breaks.
    blocking false

    highlight row1 row2 row3 row4 duration 2.5

    highlight prizes duration 2.5

A: The textbook rule prices a bet by its expected value: (@highlight prizes duration 1.4@) each prize time (@highlight r1_c r2_c r3_c r4_c duration 1.4@)s its chance, all added up.
    draw h_w h_rule2

A: Heads on t (@highlight r1_c r1_p duration 2@)he first toss has a chanc (@draw w1@)e of one half, and pays two dollars: that row is worth one dollar.

A: (@highlight r2_c r2_p duration 2@) Heads on the se (@draw w2@)cond toss: one quarter, times four dollars. One dollar again.

A: And the (@highlight r3_c duration 1.5@) third row: a (@draw w3@) chance of one (@highlight r3_p duration 1.5@) eighth, a prize of eight dollars.

    ask "What is the third row worth?" on w3 others ["8","3","64"] store tile voice a
        right: One dollar: an eighth of eight.
        wrong: Take one eighth of the prize.

A: One dollar. (@draw w4@) The chances halve exactly as fast as the prizes double, so every row is worth the same dollar.

A: And the rows never stop, because the coin can always land tails once more.
    highlight r5 effect circle

A: So the sum is on (@draw sum@)e, plus one, plus one, forever: the expected payout is infinite.

A: By that rule any price i (@draw anyprice@)s a bargain: a thousand dollars, a million, your house.

A: Yet hardly anyone would pay even twenty dollars. Daniel Bernoulli said much the same: any sensible person, he wrote, would gladly sell the chance for twenty ducats.
    highlight you

    clear

    card title "Three innocent steps"

A: So here is the argument in three st (@draw s1@)eps: a bet is worth its average payo (@draw sa1 s2@)ut; this avera (@draw sa2 s3@)ge is infinite; so pay any price.

A: Each step looks innocent and the conclusion is absu (@draw which@)rd, so one step must be wrong. Which one?

    erase which

A: Start with what really happens when you pl (@draw play_box play_line_1@)ay. Half of all games end on the first toss, with two dollars.

A: (@draw play_line_2@) Three games in four pay four dollars or less. To win more tha (@draw play_line_3@)n a thousand, the first nine tosses must all be tails: one ga (@highlight play_line_3 duration 1.8@)me in five hundred and twelve.

    erase play

A: The infinite average lives in prizes so rare and so huge you'll never see them, and someone would have to pay them.
    highlight s2

A: Say the  (@draw bank@)casino has a billion dollars, and that is the most it can ever pay.

    ask "With at most $1 billion to pay out, what is the game's average payout now?" estimate.min 0 estimate.max 100 estimate.value 30.9 estimate.unit dollars store g stage own default "50" voice a
        right: Close: you said {g}; it's about {g.true}.
        wrong: You said {g}; it's about {g.true}.

    erase estimate_1

A: About thirty-one dollars. Every row up to a billion is still wort (@draw rows30@)h a dollar, but only about thirty rows fit.
    draw b_base b_title bk1

A: Make the casino a thousand times richer, a (@draw bk2@) trillion dollars, and the game is worth only about forty-one.

A: So step two needs a bank (@draw x_s2 nobank@) with infinite money; critics said as much in the 1700s.

A: But Bernoulli saw a deeper problem. Even against a real billionaire, would you pay thirty dollars for a game that usually pays two?
    highlight s1

    clear

    card title "Bernoulli's answer"

A: (@draw bern@) His answer: a dollar is not always worth (@draw ax ax_x@) a dollar. A thousand ducats mean far more to a pauper than to a rich man.

    erase bern

A: He calle (@draw ax_y@)d what money is worth to you its utility: it  (@draw ucurve@)grows with wealth, but more and more slowly.

A: On his curve, every  (@draw d1 d1_l d2 d2_l st1 st1_l@)doubling adds the same step (@draw d4 d4_l st2 st2_l@) up: two dollars to four c (@draw d8 d8_l st4 st4_l@)ounts as  (@draw d16 d16_l st8 st8_l@)much as four to eight, or a million to two million.

A: The game's prizes double row by row, so each row climbs just one more step, while its chance keeps halving.
    highlight steps_up

A: For someone with nothing else, add i (@draw usum_box usum_line_1@)t up: a half times one step, a quarter times two, an eighth times three, and so on.

A: That sum doesn't run awa (@draw usum_line_2@)y: it settles at exactly two step (@highlight d1 d1_l duration 1.5@)s. Count from one dollar: (@highlight st1 st1_l duration 1.5@) two dollars is one (@highlight st2 st2_l duration 1.5@) step up, four dollars two (@draw four_line four_l four_l2@). So the game is worth four dollars.

    erase usum

A: Count in the money you already have, and the price c (@draw ce1@)reeps up slowly: about eleven dollars (@draw ce2@) if you have a thousand, about twenty-one for a millionaire.

    pause 1.2

    clear

    card title "What it changed"

A: (@draw steps x_s2 nobank@) So, back to the three steps. B (@fade x_s2 nobank to 0.45@)ernoulli blamed (@highlight s1 duration 2@) the first, and that is the answer most textbooks gi (@draw x_s1@)ve: a bet isn't worth its average payout in dollars.

A: It is worth the ave (@draw eu_a eu@)rage of what those dollars mean to you, its expected utility. In 1944  (@highlight eu duration 2@)von Neumann and Morgenstern gave that idea the form economists still use.

A: It explains insuran (@draw ins@)ce. Bernoulli's own example was a merchant shipping good (@draw ship@)s by sea to St Petersburg: paying a premium makes sense when losing the car (@highlight ship duration 2@)go would hurt far more than its price.

A: It isn't the last word. In 1934 Karl Menge (@draw menger@)r showed that if the prizes grow fast enough, even Bernoulli's curve gi (@draw q_mark@)ves an infinite value again.

A: So some say utility mu (@draw ceil@)st have a ceiling, others that cha (@draw tiny@)nces so tiny should be ignored; the argument still runs.

A: What survives is Bernoulli's point: a dollar is worth less the more you already have, so huge, rare prizes count for far less than their size.
    highlight eu

    quiz "A $10 ticket wins $1 million with a chance of 1 in 50,000, otherwise nothing. Its average payout is $20. Why might a sensible person with $1,000 refuse?" voice a
        right: The million counts for less: with $1,000 to their name, the sure ten dollars weighs more than a long shot whose dollars each mean less and less to them.
        wrong: The average really is in the buyer's favour. Think about what a million dollars is worth to someone who has only $1,000.
        * A $10 profit isn't worth it
        + The million counts for less
        * Rare wins never happen

    erase ship ins menger q_mark ceil tiny

A: (@draw coin2@) So, would you pay a fortune for a game with an infinite average? Not if  (@draw end_you@)dollars mean less the more you have: even a millionaire  (@draw end_mil@)should stop at about twenty-one.

