      ms: 5560
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:ma6x8o'
    '|a||Yet hardly anyone would pay even twenty dollars. Daniel Bernoulli said much the same: any sensible person, he wrote, would gladly sell the chance for twenty ducats.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 10280
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:1wqjxei'
    '|a||So here is the argument in three steps: a bet is worth its average payout; this average is infinite; so pay any price.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 9400
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:1vkp6j2'
    '|a||Each step looks innocent and the conclusion is absurd, so one step must be wrong. Which one?':
      mp3: 
      ms: 7400
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:orkum7'
    '|a||Start with what really happens when you play. Half of all games end on the first toss, with two dollars.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 6480
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:18vlxvk'
    '|a||Three games in four pay four dollars or less. To win more than a thousand, the first nine tosses must all be tails: one game in five hundred and twelve.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 10840
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:1m6lmve'
    '|a||The infinite average lives in prizes so rare and so huge you''ll never see them, and someone would have to pay them.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 7640
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:1ykg30s'
    '|a||Say the casino has a billion dollars, and that is the most it can ever pay.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 4760
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:a1ahw1'
    '|a||With at most $1 billion to pay out, what is the game''s average payout now?':
      mp3: 
      ms: 4880
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:1q4fl9r'
    '|a||About thirty-one dollars. Every row up to a billion is still worth a dollar, but only about thirty rows fit.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 6600
      voice: gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed
    '|a||Make the casino a thousand times richer, a trillion dollars, and the game is worth only about forty-one.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 6280
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:zbnech'
    '|a||So step two needs a bank with infinite money; critics said as much in the 1700s.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 5760
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:j0pdix'
    '|a||But Bernoulli saw a deeper problem. Even against a real billionaire, would you pay thirty dollars for a game that usually pays two?':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8200
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:1mrkqud'
    '|a||His answer: a dollar is not always worth a dollar. A thousand ducats mean far more to a pauper than to a rich man.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8200
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:1bzgscy'
    '|a||He called what money is worth to you its utility: it grows with wealth, but more and more slowly.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 6760
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:18s6dq6'
    '|a||On his curve, every doubling adds the same step up: two dollars to four counts as much as four to eight, or a million to two million.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8080
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:169rs9g'
    '|a||The game''s prizes double row by row, so each row climbs just one more step, while its chance keeps halving.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 6760
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:u2lugp'
    '|a||For someone with nothing else, add it up: a half times one step, a quarter times two, an eighth times three, and so on.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 7960
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:1o27she'
    '|a||That sum doesn''t run away: it settles at exactly two steps. Count from one dollar: two dollars is one step up, four dollars two. So the game is worth four dollars.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 10640
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:1frfdnp'
    '|a||Count in the money you already have, and the price creeps up slowly: about eleven dollars if you have a thousand, about twenty-one for a millionaire.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 8880
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:28ppne'
    '|a||So, back to the three steps. Bernoulli blamed the first, and that is the answer most textbooks give: a bet isn''t worth its average payout in dollars.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 9280
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:7uubz2'
    '|a||It is worth the average of what those dollars mean to you, its expected utility. In 1944 von Neumann and Morgenstern gave that idea the form economists still use.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 11320
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:mak574'
    '|a||It explains insurance. Bernoulli''s own example was a merchant shipping goods by sea to St Petersburg: paying a premium makes sense when losing the cargo would hurt far more than its price.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 10640
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:m0xmlp'
    '|a||It isn''t the last word. In 1934 Karl Menger showed that if the prizes grow fast enough, even Bernoulli''s curve gives an infinite value again.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 10080
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:1s5w910'
    '|a||So some say utility must have a ceiling, others that chances so tiny should be ignored; the argument still runs.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 7120
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:e5maj5'
    '|a||What survives is Bernoulli''s point: a dollar is worth less the more you already have, so huge, rare prizes count for far less than their size.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 10000
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:1x4mh4y'
    '|a||A $10 ticket wins $1 million with a chance of 1 in 50,000, otherwise nothing. Its average payout is $20. Why might a sensible person with $1,000 refuse?':
      mp3: 
      ms: 12600
      voice: 'gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed #ctx:1dkpd8g'
    '|a||The million counts for less: with $1,000 to their name, the sure ten dollars weighs more than a long shot whose dollars each mean less and less to them.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 9840
      voice: gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed
    '|a||So, would you pay a fortune for a game with an infinite average? Not if dollars mean less the more you have: even a millionaire should stop at about twenty-one.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 9680
      voice: gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed
    '|a||Next: One box or two? Newcomb''s problem':
      mp3: 
      ms: 3840
      voice: gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed
    '|a||One dollar: an eighth of eight.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 2440
      voice: gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed
    '|a||Take one eighth of the prize.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 2280
      voice: gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed
    '|a||The average really is in the buyer''s favour. Think about what a million dollars is worth to someone who has only $1,000.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 7280
      voice: gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed
    '|a||Yes.':
